  "notes": "{砂漠|さばく} refers to a vast, arid region with little rainfall and sparse vegetation. Japan has no true deserts, so this word is mainly encountered in geography, news about foreign countries, and figurative expressions.\n\nETYMOLOGY:\n{砂|さ} (sand) + {漠|ばく} (vague, vast).\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- サハラ{砂漠|さばく}: Sahara Desert\n- {砂漠化|さばくか}: desertification\n- {砂漠|さばく}の{中|なか}: in the middle of the desert\n- {砂漠|さばく}{地帯|ちたい}: desert zone/region\n\nUSAGE:\nSometimes used metaphorically to describe emptiness or barrenness: {心|こころ}の{砂漠|さばく} (emotional wasteland), {文化|ぶんか}の{砂漠|さばく} (cultural desert).\n\nWATCH OUT:\nDo not confuse with the verb {裁|さば}く (to judge), which shares the same reading but has different kanji and meaning.",
